{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqpv6qx0gzv","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"cmm onboard makes subconscious KB beat grep on code nav at one tenth tokens but KB sync embed is the hard bottleneck","summary":"Definitive result for the cmm-as-onboard-engine experiment on the 13-query code-nav corpus. Clean measurement over an 863-of-1321 (65 percent coverage) cmm-onboarded scratch KB (daemon healthy, 0 empty searches): subconscious /search mean_recall 0.346, mean_tokens 310, hit 7/13. Versus un-onboarded subconscious 0.045 (2/13) that is a 7.7x recall lift; it BEATS the naive grep+read arm recall (0.321) at ~1/10 the tokens (310 vs 3026); still below the codebase-memory-mcp DIRECT structural arm (0.506, 327 tok, 10/13) because of 65 percent coverage plus RAG-over-note-summaries being fuzzier than direct symbol-name graph search. Misses (q01 createWorktree, q08 mergeBranch) are functions cmm extracted but that were never injected (the missing 35 percent). HARD BOTTLENECK CONFIRMED: the orchestrator KB cannot bulk-ingest — synchronous per-note embedding on POST /overlay/note serializes, so even a throttled client (concurrency 3, 150ms paced, health-guarded) degraded /health and auto-aborted at 863 notes; a fully unthrottled run (concurrency 16) hung the daemon for ~28s. Client-side throttling cannot fix server-side embed serialization. IMPLICATION for build-vs-wrap: the first-party cmm-style onboard is two pieces and the second is the load-bearing one — (1) a JS/TS AST extractor (Babel/acorn, reuse onboard-mine-structure.js scaffolding), and (2) a BATCHED/ASYNC bulk-embed ingest path so onboarding 1k+ symbols does not take down the daemon. Extraction is trivial; ingest throughput is the real engineering work.","category":"project","tags":["benchmark","codebase-memory-mcp","onboard","kb","result","throughput","bottleneck"],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-22T23:46:28.164Z","vec":[-0.031200937926769257,-0.05342169851064682,0.03386613354086876,0.0401231050491333,